course: dictionary
lesson: 1049
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Borrowing means taking something that belongs to someone else, with their permission, and giving it back later. The context helps you know which meaning is right. «Borrowing is the same idea at school: things belong to their owner, so you ask first, look after them, give them back, and own up if something goes wrong.» The words ask first and give them back are clues: this borrowing is about lending and returning things.
explain: Sometimes the clues point somewhere else. «Banks check whether a borrower is creditworthy, which means likely to pay back, by looking at income and past borrowing.» Here a bank and pay back tell you that borrowing is about money. You get money now and must pay it back later, often with extra called interest.
explain: Borrowing can also be a word taken from another language, such as «café» from French. «English has many borrowings from French, Latin, Norse and Hindi.» In maths, borrowing means taking ten from the next column in subtraction. When you meet a word with many meanings, swap each meaning into the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
fact: Borrowing means taking something that belongs to someone else, with permission, and giving it back.
fact: Borrowing money means you must pay it back, often with extra called interest.
fact: A borrowing can be a word taken from another language, like «café» from French.
fact: In maths subtraction, borrowing means taking ten from the next column, also called regrouping.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: Sam asks to borrow Ruby's pencil and promises to give it back at lunch. What is borrowing in this case? | Buying a new pencil for yourself | *Taking something that belongs to someone else, with permission, to give back later | Keeping something you found on the floor
exercise: «Banks check whether a borrower is creditworthy.» What does borrowing mean when it is about money? | *Getting money that must be paid back, often with extra | Counting coins in a jar | Giving money away to friends
exercise: Which word is a borrowing from French? | happy | *café | blue
exercise: 
test: In maths subtraction, what does borrowing mean? | *Taking ten from the next column | Adding two numbers together | Taking a book from the library
test: «Things belong to their owner, so you ask first.» What should you do before borrowing something? | Hide it until you forget | *Ask first | Take it quietly
exam: «Banks check whether a borrower is creditworthy, which means likely to pay back.» Which meaning of borrowing fits this sentence? | *Getting money that must be paid back, often with interest | Giving money away for free | Taking a word from another language
